Pendant: How It Works

Overview
Pendant turns a short text prompt like "sad lo-fi, 80 BPM" into a playable 16-bar MIDI song, written by a band of AI agents. Each agent plays one role, the way musicians in a real band do: a composer plans the song, then drums, bass, chords and melody each write their own part.

Two safety nets keep the music correct. A plain-code validator (no AI) checks every note against music-theory rules. A critic agent listens to the whole song and sends specific bars back to the agent responsible.

Every run writes three files into outputs/<prompt>/:

song.mid: the song, ready to import into any DAW (GarageBand, Ableton, FL Studio) or MIDI player

song.json: the composer's blueprint plus every note each instrument played

report.json: how the run went (model calls per agent, retries, repairs, critic rounds)

The project runs with OpenAI models (default gpt-5-mini) or fully offline with rule-based stand-in musicians, so it works without an API key. It can also run inside Flower as an AgentApp, covered in its own section below.

Source: Pranjal101Shrivastava/pendant_music, branch codex/complete-pendant. Python 3.11+, version 0.2.0.

How a song gets made
A run goes composer → four instruments in order → critic → files. Each instrument hears the parts written before it, and nothing reaches the MIDI file without passing the validator. The diagram after the steps shows both loops.

Composer writes the blueprint. From the prompt it picks key, mode, tempo, time signature and sections (for example Intro, Verse, Chorus, Outro). Each section gets one chord per bar, an energy level from 0 to 1, and a list of which instruments play. It also picks a General MIDI sound for bass, chords and melody. The validator checks the blueprint: every chord must use only notes of the key.

Drums write the groove first. The drummer starts from a genre template (pop/rock, lo-fi, funk, jazz, ambient) and varies it. Every hit sits on the 16th-note grid.

Bass locks to the kick drum. It sees the drum part and plays one note at a time.

Chords fill the harmony. At most 6 notes sound at once, following the blueprint's chord for each bar.

Melody goes on top. It hears everything before it and plays one note at a time.

Validator checks every part. It checks pitch range, key, song length, overlapping notes, one-note-at-a-time for bass and melody, and the drum grid. Instruments may only play in sections that list them. Each error names the bar and beat, and goes back to the same agent for up to 2 retries. If retries run out, code repairs the best attempt (moves notes into range or key, trims overlaps).

Critic reviews the whole song. It scores four areas from 1 to 5: groove, harmonic fit, variation and balance. It also gets objective measurements from code (core/analysis.py). It either accepts or names at most 3 issues, each with one agent and a bar range.

Flagged agents rewrite only the flagged bars. Everything else in their part stays the same. The critic runs at most 2 rounds. The best-scoring version wins, even if a revision made things worse.

Files are written. song.mid, song.json and report.json, plus song.wav if audio is on.

A global budget caps every run at 60 model calls by default, including retries and critic rounds.

The inner loop sends validator errors back to the same musician; the outer loop sends critic issues back for flagged bars only.

Autonomous mode (version 2)
--mode autonomous turns on three extras. The core pieces stay the same.

Switch

What changes

Self-check (7a)

Each instrument can call a check_my_part tool to run the validator on a draft before answering

Orchestration (7b)

The composer chooses which instruments play and in what order, instead of the fixed drums → bass → chords → melody

Feedback (7c)

Each instrument can send a note back ("this tempo is too fast for the bass line"). The composer may revise the blueprint once, and the parts are rewritten

