Check the integration without a key
python scripts/flower_smoke.py starts a real local SuperLink plus a fake OpenAI server, then runs an offline song and a mock-OpenAI song. It checks 11 music requests, 4 check_my_part round trips and a valid MIDI file. On 2026-09-30 it printed PASS for both runs (Linux/macOS only).

Tests, limits and differences from the plan
All 107 tests pass (python -m pytest, run on 2026-09-30 with Python 3.11) with no API key and no paid calls. They cover the validator rules, music theory, MIDI writing, agents, critic, pipeline, both OpenAI transports (with a fake client) and the Flower app.

Limits to know

Real OpenAI quality is untested by the tests. The five demo songs came from OpenAI runs, but the test suite and smoke check use fake clients.

Audio needs your own tools. --audio needs a FluidSynth binary and a licensed General MIDI .sf2 SoundFont, neither bundled. Without them the MIDI is still written and a message explains why the WAV was skipped.

Hard caps by design. Up to 2 validator retries per agent, 2 critic rounds, 3 issues per round, 1 blueprint revision, 60 calls per run. Setting a higher value fails at startup.

Chords must stay in the key. The composer may not use borrowed or secondary-dominant chords, which limits some genres (jazz especially).

No licence to reuse. LICENSE keeps all rights with the owner; it exists only because Flower's bundle format requires the file.

Flower is pinned to 1.39.x, because the AgentApp API is new.



